  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A three-ton stone lion flew over Beijing's city wall. The thirtieth of May, 1626, mid-morning, in the reign of the Tianqi Emperor.",
        "visual_query": "stone lion statue beijing",
        "visual_fallback": "stone lion"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Everything inside two kilometres was gone. The ground at the centre dropped six metres, and full-grown trees came down out at Miyun, far outside the city.",
        "visual_query": "collapsed brick rubble street",
        "visual_fallback": "brick rubble"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Witnesses described a coloured cloud shaped like a black mushroom, hanging there for hours. What flattens a capital city in 1626?",
        "visual_query": "dark storm cloud sky",
        "visual_fallback": "cloud sky"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The centre of it was the Wanggongchang armoury, one of six imperial gunpowder works, normally staffed by eighty people. As many as twenty thousand died.",
        "visual_query": "forbidden city rooftops beijing",
        "visual_fallback": "chinese rooftops"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Gunpowder is the obvious answer and still an unproven one. Historians have floated a meteor airburst too. The emperor's seven-month-old heir died after it.",
        "visual_query": "black gunpowder grains closeup",
        "visual_fallback": "gunpowder powder"
      }
    ],
